  "OverallSummary": "This case involves a willful violation of Regulation BI's Care Obligation by John Wigmore Reilly III, who recommended and executed 25 short-term switches of Class A mutual fund shares for two senior retired customers between July 2018 and September 2021. The violations occurred across two regulatory periods: 22 switches before June 30, 2020 (violating FINRA Rule 2111) and 3 switches after June 30, 2020 (violating Regulation BI's Care Obligation). The Care Obligation violations included: (1) failing to exercise reasonable diligence, care, and skill when recommending the mutual fund switches; (2) failing to consider the customers' investment objectives and time horizons; (3) failing to consider costs associated with the recommendations; and (4) failing to consider reasonably available alternatives that would have achieved the customers' objectives at lower cost, such as switching within the same fund family. The switches involved Class A mutual funds, which typically have substantial front-end loads and are intended for long-term investment, with an average holding period of only 229-273 days. Both customers were over 65, retired, with moderate risk tolerances. The customers collectively paid $40,972.50 in excessive sales fees, of which $31,675.14 is being returned as restitution. Reilly was also fined $5,000 and suspended for three months."
